---
name: reporivet-main
description: Route a substantive repository goal through AGENTS.md, current authority, one durable active Plan, bounded Sub Agents, integration, and evidence judgment. Use when acting as Main for planning, dispatch, integration, handoff, or Plan lifecycle work.
---

Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/README.md`, and the authority needed for intent, scope, acceptance, and decomposition. The default onboarding entry point is integrated `reporivet setup`; `reporivet init` remains structure-only, and the lower-level `reporivet define` flow remains available.

Before the first dispatch for a substantive goal, perform this first-Plan handoff:

1. Search both `docs/exec-plans/active/` and `docs/exec-plans/completed/` for ordinary Markdown Plans (`*.md`) matching the goal; do not treat `_template.md` or hidden state as a Plan.
2. If exactly one matching active Plan exists, resume exactly one matching active Plan. Stop if multiple active Plans match. Completed Plans are historical and are not resumed.
3. Otherwise choose the lowest unused current-year numeric ID across both directories, and copy `_template.md` (`docs/exec-plans/_template.md`) to a new active Plan path. Never overwrite/reuse an ID.
4. Keep state in visible Markdown. Main—not package runtime—creates/resumes Plans; never use a Plan CLI/hidden state/automatic dispatcher/closure.

Dynamic procedure Skill ownership is project-owned. Only complete Confirmed structured procedure records can produce an instruction-only `.claude/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md` through resumed `reporivet setup`. Do not pre-generate a project-specific Skill. Keep the Skill instruction-only and least privilege, with frontmatter limited to `name` and `description`; no procedure is inferred or executed. Preserve and never delete any existing differing, stale, or arbitrary Skill.

For every milestone classified as broad, apply this common installed-project rule:

`T<n> (broad milestone) -> T<n>-A/B/C/... (owned child packets, all ready leaves dispatched concurrently) -> T<n>-I (integration) -> T<n>-V1/V2/... (parallel fresh verification)`

Broad or multi-part roots default to `Role: Task Owner` and `May delegate: yes`; narrow or inherently serial root milestones remain direct non-delegating leaves. Main dispatches independent root Owners concurrently. This rule does not apply to inherently single or serial milestones. If a child is itself broad, only a packet marked `Role: Task Owner` and `May delegate: yes` may run its predeclared bounded descendants such as `T<n>-A-1`; ordinary leaf Agents do not delegate.

Main serializes a finite accepted child manifest inside the approved envelope before resuming that Owner. Main alone reviews and records the accepted child rows and complete packets in the active Plan. Only the resumed Owner dispatches its complete declared dependency-ready descendants through the host's native Agent execution; no newly discovered child or boundary bypasses this checkpoint. Ordinary leaves, integrations, and verification leaves never delegate.

Main owns the overall task tree: the original goal, observable acceptance criteria, non-goals, dependencies, hierarchical Task Packets, dispatch, integration order, decisions, current checkpoint, exact next action, documentation impact, verification sufficiency, and terminal Plan transition. Main alone serializes Plan edits and owns final integration—the final repository integration.

Keep the Main context small. Using only the host's native Agent execution, dispatch the complete dependency-ready root set concurrently for each broad milestone; within each root's declared subtree, its resumed Owner dispatches the complete dependency-ready leaf set concurrently. Preserve declared linear order for inherently single or serial milestones. Owners and descendants inherit scope, acceptance, non-goals, protected paths, child budget, frozen interfaces, and the exact baseline; they cannot broaden those boundaries. Concurrent mutable siblings require disjoint allowed-write sets and separate worktrees, each rooted at the exact baseline. Each Owner performs review, tests, and Owner-local aggregation within its subtree and returns compact evidence; that local aggregation is not Main integration or fresh verification.

Reporivet installs no scheduler, task store, lease, lock, or automatic dispatcher; it also installs no dispatcher, runtime, hidden state, task DB, runner, generated CI, Gate, evidence archive, deployment engine, or automatic closure. Before dispatch, name the outcome, non-goals, exact reads, allowed writes, protected paths, acceptance criteria, project-owned verification commands, stop conditions, and compact return evidence. Route siblings through their explicit integration node, then dispatch fresh candidate-specific Verification Agents that depend on the integrated candidate. They are read-only, nonrepairing, and nondelegating; implementation or Owner tests do not self-approve, and later candidate mutation invalidates their evidence.

Ask Sub Agents for Plan-ready evidence instead of concurrent Plan writes. For meaningful behavior changes, use separate implementation and fresh verification contexts. Do not normally repeat a Verification Sub's detailed command run.

At each durable checkpoint, update the active Plan so a new Main can continue without reconstructing chat history. Move it to `completed/` only with actual status `complete`, `cancelled`, or `superseded` after the outcome is terminal.
